# Design direction

> Purpose: the dev needs no design knowledge. The AI chooses the look from the concept and writes it into the project files. Structure stays locked in `ui-styling.mdc`. The pack rules never name a palette.

## Flow (`format.mdc`: Ask → Recommend → Decide → Write → Confirm)

1. **Ask** for constraints only: brand already decided (logo, brand color, fonts), light/dark preference, tone words, references. Dev-provided values **win**.
2. **Recommend** 2–3 directions as `REC-design-n` blocks, one **Recommended**. Each direction carries:
   | Part | Content |
   |------|---------|
   | Style | One name from the style list below, and why it fits **this** concept |
   | Palette | Primary, neutral ramp, surface/card/border, success/warn/danger — hex + role + measured contrast |
   | Mode | Light / dark / both (recommend both when users work long or at night) |
   | Type | UI/body + display pairing, weights, base size, line-height |
   | Shape & density | Radius, shadow/border style, spacing rhythm, compact vs airy |
   | Icons & motion | One icon key from `icons-states.mdc` (`material` · `fontawesome` · `flaticon` · `sf-symbols` · `lottie`). If the installer did not choose, recommend from the concept. Motion character |
   | Feel | One paragraph: what the main screen feels like |
3. **Decide** — the AI takes the **Recommended** direction and records it `AI-chosen`, in the same turn. Do not wait for the dev to name a style, a palette, or a font. A brand they already stated still wins. They may later say "warmer" or pick another option; then update the same files.
4. **Write** that decision into `frontend-design/SKILL.md` (style name, tokens, type, icons), the Tailwind theme, and Profile → **Design direction**. Status `DRAFT` until they approve it; the look is still in those files, not only in chat.

## Style (the AI picks one)

A style name is a spec to build from, not a mood word. The AI picks the style per page and per layout class. It does not ask the dev to name it. One view uses one style. Do not mix two styles on the same screen.

Each route in `route-layouts.mdc` records three lines — **Style — mobile**, **Style — tablet**, **Style — desktop** — each with a name from this list and the concrete rules for that class (type, surface, chrome). “Same as mobile” or “clean and modern” is not a style line. A landing may be **Bold campaign** while an admin list is **Utilitarian**. They still share the project tokens.

| Style | Follow this |
|-------|-------------|
| **Editorial** | Serif display, sans body, wide measure, large headlines, hairline rules, few colors, quiet cards, no heavy shadow |
| **Futuristic** | Geometric sans, tight label tracking, hairline borders, cool near-black surfaces, one sharp accent, restrained glow on the primary control only, mono for figures |
| **Soft product** | Rounded sans, large radius, warm off-white, soft shadow, friendly empty states, one calm accent |
| **Warm commerce** | Rounded cards, price as the strongest text, warm neutrals, one appetizing accent, clear photo crops |
| **Utilitarian** | Compact density, tabular numbers, neutral surfaces, accent only on the primary action, sticky table head, no decoration |
| **Calm clinical** | Cool neutrals, generous space, plain labels, no decoration, status color only for success and danger |
| **Bold campaign** | Display type at hero scale, one saturated accent, full-bleed bands, body still at reading size and contrast |

If none fits, name the closest and write what was adjusted. Do not invent a style that breaks the four goals below.

## The recommendation is the design

The style lines on the route are what P2 builds. They are not a note for later.

- A page that looks like an unstyled form or a default card stack is not done.
- Mobile, tablet, and desktop each follow their own style line. Desktop is not a stretched phone screen with the same padding.

## Every recommendation must satisfy four goals

| Goal | Requirements |
|------|--------------|
| **UX-first** | Clear hierarchy, one primary action per view, familiar patterns, visible feedback, short paths, targets ≥ ~44px |
| **Modern** | Restrained: whitespace, soft radius, hairline borders/soft shadows, 1–2 typefaces, one icon style; no dated gradients, skeuomorphism, or badge soup |
| **Easy on the eyes** | Rules below |
| **Worth coming back to** | Rules below |

### Easy on the eyes
- No pure `#000` on pure `#fff` — near-black on soft off-white (light-gray on dark-gray in dark mode)
- Text contrast WCAG AA (4.5:1 body, 3:1 large/UI) without going harsh
- One accent for actions; large surfaces neutral; avoid big saturated fills and vibrating pairs
- Dark mode: dark-gray surfaces (not `#000`), slightly desaturated accents, elevation by lighter surface
- Body ≥ 16px, `leading-relaxed`, line length ~60–75 chars, weights 400/500/600
- Whitespace separates groups; no looping/flashing motion in content areas

### Worth coming back to (quality, not tricks)
- Fast and smooth: skeletons, optimistic UI where safe, lazy load, no layout shift
- Familiar and consistent: returning users never relearn
- Low-friction return: remember theme/filters/last view; resume where they left off
- Small delight: 100–250ms micro-interactions, friendly empty states, clear success moments
- Trust: honest copy, errors with a next step, undo over scary confirms
- No dark patterns: no manipulative streaks, fake urgency, or hidden opt-outs

## Do not
- Present one option only, or several without a Recommended
- Choose colors by taste alone — show contrast ratios and the concept-based reason
- Hardcode the palette outside the Tailwind theme, or change the approved direction silently
- Use a generic “clean and modern” note as the design. The written direction is specific to this concept
- Leave any page on a basic, junior layout once the direction is chosen
